  "body": {
    "id": "thm:full-branch-geometry",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "On every primitive stratum defined by \\(\\mathrm{def{:}branch\\mbox{-}system}\\), each active cell's complete-event conditional envelope \\(M_j\\) and the global envelope \\(F\\) are finite maxima and minima of analytic or Puiseux-continuable branches. The global maximizing holdout set consists exactly of the retained interval endpoints, projected branch endpoints, stationary branch roots, branch intersections, and flat maximizing intervals attaining \\(V^{\\star}\\); for each such \\(\\rho\\), the full weight set is \\(\\prod_{j=(C,s)\\in J}\\{\\theta_j:\\Pi_j(\\rho,\\theta_j,\\ell_s)\\ge V^{\\star}\\}\\). This characterization is necessary and sufficient in all positive, negative, zero, and mixed-sign \\(\\Delta\\) regimes, with every \\(\\Pi_j\\) conditioned on \\(E_{n,C}(\\rho)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "C",
      "S",
      "EC",
      "M",
      "J",
      "F",
      "Vstar",
      "rho",
      "theta",
      "Pi",
      "ell",
      "Delta"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:positive-compact-alternatives",
      "ass:primitive-compactness",
      "lem:derived-residual-scale",
      "thm:least-coordinate-reduction",
      "thm:weight-side",
      "thm:global-endpoint",
      "thm:negative-interior",
      "def:design-objective",
      "def:branch-system",
      "lem:semianalytic-finite-stratification"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Fix a primitive vector on a stratum of \\(\\mathrm{def{:}branch\\mbox{-}system}\\), an active \\(j=(C,s)\\), and put\n\\[\np_j(\\rho,\\vartheta)=\\Pi_j(\\rho,\\vartheta,\\ell_s),\n\\qquad D_j=I\\times[0,\\pi/2].\n\\]\nThe residual-scale lemma gives \\(v_j^2\\ge\\rho_0\\).  Define\n\\[\nH_s(q,r)=\\frac1{Q(c_s)}\\int_{c_s}^{\\infty}\n\\phi(z)Q\\!\\left(\\frac{q-rz}{\\sqrt{1-r^2}}\\right)\\,dz.\n\\]\nOn the compact correlation range, differentiation under the integral is dominated by a polynomial times a Gaussian density, and\n\\[\n\\partial_qH_s(q,r)=\n-\\frac1{Q(c_s)\\sqrt{1-r^2}}\n\\int_{c_s}^{\\infty}\\phi(z)\n\\phi\\!\\left(\\frac{q-rz}{\\sqrt{1-r^2}}\\right)\\,dz<0.\n\\]\nThe same domination of local power series makes \\(H_s\\) real analytic.  The analytic implicit-function theorem makes its unique solution \\(H_s(q,r)=\\alpha\\) analytic in \\(r\\), and a change of variable at the moving lower limit then proves that \\(p_j\\) is analytic on a neighborhood of \\(D_j\\).\n\nLet \\(Z_j=\\{(\\rho,\\vartheta)\\in D_j:\\partial_\\vartheta p_j=0\\}\\).  Its defining analytic function cannot vanish on a two-dimensional open set: analytic continuation would make \\(p_j(\\rho,\\cdot)\\) constant, contradicting the weight-side theorem in each of the three discrepancy signs.  Hence \\(Z_j\\) has dimension at most one.  The cited semianalytic-stratification lemma, applied compatibly with projection and the boundary, decomposes it into finitely many points and analytic arcs.  After finitely many cuts, every rank-one projected arc is an analytic graph \\(\\vartheta=\\vartheta_{j\\ell}(\\rho)\\), with Puiseux continuation at its recorded ramified endpoints; rank-zero arcs project to retained points.  Angular endpoint graphs are also included.\n\nFermat's rule and compactness of the angle interval now give, on each projected stratum,\n\\[\nM_j(\\rho)=\\max_{\\ell\\in L_j}g_{j\\ell}(\\rho),\n\\qquad\ng_{j\\ell}(\\rho)=p_j(\\rho,\\vartheta_{j\\ell}(\\rho)),\n\\]\nfor a finite branch set including angular endpoints.  Refine simultaneously over the finite set \\(J\\) by every nonidentical crossing, nontransverse crossing, candidate-value tie, and projected endpoint.  Identical branches and flat branches are retained symbolically.  The semianalytic lemma makes the refinement finite.  On each remaining open interval, branch order is fixed, so\n\\[\nF(\\rho)=\\min_{j\\in J}M_j(\\rho)\n\\]\nequals one analytic or Puiseux-continuable branch, with all ties retained.\n\nAn interior maximizer on a nonconstant active branch is a stationary root; a constant maximizing branch contributes its whole flat interval; every other maximizer is an endpoint or recorded transition.  Conversely, every retained candidate attaining \\(V^\\star\\) is a global maximizer by definition.  This proves the necessary-and-sufficient holdout list.\n\nFor weights, cellwise maximization gives\n\\[\n\\max_{\\theta\\in[0,\\pi/2]^J}\\min_{j\\in J}p_j(\\rho,\\theta_j)\n=\\min_{j\\in J}M_j(\\rho)=F(\\rho).\n\\]\nThe least-coordinate theorem transfers this identity to the original compact alternative set.  Therefore the full weight set at an optimal \\(\\rho\\) is exactly\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=(C,s)\\in J}\n\\{\\theta_j\\in[0,\\pi/2]:\\Pi_j(\\rho,\\theta_j,\\ell_s)\\ge V^\\star\\}.\n\\]\nOnly finiteness of \\(J\\), inherited from fixed finite \\(S\\), was used.  The sign theorem restricts branches in positive, zero, negative, and mixed-sign regimes; the endpoint theorem and the two-stratum interior witness certify that both boundary and interior geometries actually occur.",
    "justification": "The finite branch system is necessary and sufficient for positive, zero, negative, and mixed signs over finite \\(J\\).",
    "gap": "Model-based policy programs do not characterize this continuous set-valued conditional-power argmax.",
    "consumer": "All ties, flat intervals, and slack-cell weight continua remain auditable.",
